# Shipyard TODO

## [SHP-000] Foundation

> **Goal:** Establish a stable, usable CLI foundation: parsing, command discovery, registry loading, configuration, error handling, and the infrastructure required by later commands.
>
> **Release target:** `SHP-000` is complete when Shipyard can reliably start, parse input, resolve and load commands, handle global flags, execute commands, report errors, and return consistent exit statuses.

* [x] Implement the CLI parser.

  * Tokenize command-line input.
  * Normalize words, options, and flags.
  * Parse command arguments through `GrammarRegistry`.
  * Produce `ParseResult`.
  * Dispatch parsed input to the command's `run()` method.

* [x] Create the application bootstrap.

  * `__init__.py`
  * `__main__.py`
  * `cli.py`
  * Root-level exception boundary.

* [x] Create the core infrastructure.

  * `core.py`
  * `error.py`
  * `utils.py`
  * Shared types and registry structures.

* [x] Implement the command base class and command contract.

  * Command lifecycle.
  * Metadata contract.
  * Grammar contract.
  * Child-command contract.
  * Execution contract.

* [x] Implement command metadata.

  * Command name.
  * Description and help metadata.
  * Hidden commands.
  * Command directory.
  * Child-command directory.
  * Entry-class definition.

* [x] Implement command registry discovery.

  * Discover command directories.
  * Load `metadata.py`.
  * Validate `RegistryData`.
  * Detect duplicate command names.
  * Resolve command paths.
  * Validate child directories.
  * Resolve command entry classes.
  * Collect invalid-command errors without stopping discovery.

* [x] Implement lazy command loading.

  * Discover metadata without loading command implementations.
  * Load the command implementation only when selected.

* [x] Implement configuration handling.

  * Load `shipyard.toml`.
  * Merge user configuration with defaults.
  * Search upward for project configuration.
  * Create default configuration.
  * Save configuration atomically.

* [x] Implement shared file utilities.

  * Atomic file writes.
  * Safe file opening.
  * Generic list streams.
  * Python module/file loading.
  * Recursive dictionary merging.

* [x] Implement the CLI error hierarchy.

  * Base `ShipyardError`.
  * Usage errors.
  * Parser errors.
  * Unknown-command errors.
  * Invalid-input errors.
  * Command loading errors.
  * Registry errors.
  * File/configuration errors.

* [x] Implement user-facing error rendering.

  * Human-readable errors.
  * Hints.
  * Command suggestions.
  * Development traceback output.

* [x] Write focused foundation unit tests.

  * Test behavior at the level where it is owned.
  * Avoid duplicating inherited behavior across child commands.

* [/] Finalize command child-routing semantics.

  * Matching child → delegate to child.
  * No matching child → current command handles the remaining input.
  * Child commands should not duplicate root-level parsing responsibilities.

* [/] Finalize global/core flag handling.

  * Establish one authoritative definition for global flags.
  * Support `--version`.
  * Support `--help`.
  * Support `--dev`.
  * Support `--no-color`.
  * Support `--only-json`.
  * Support the planned developer traceback flag.
  * Handle global flags at the appropriate command layer.

* [/] Finalize command execution and exit-status handling.

  * Commands return integer exit statuses.
  * Establish the final execution boundary.
  * Propagate explicit command return codes.
  * Convert handled exceptions into appropriate process exit statuses.

* [ ] Define standard CLI exit-code constants.

  * Success.
  * Generic error.
  * Usage/input errors.
  * Configuration errors.
  * Internal errors.
  * OS/I/O errors.
  * Permission errors.
  * Command-not-found errors.
  * Interrupt/signal-related statuses.

* [ ] Implement exit-code mapping for Shipyard errors.

* [ ] Finalize `--version`.

  * Print the Shipyard version.
  * Do not require project configuration when only version information is requested.

* [/] Design and implement the help system.

  * Generate help from command metadata and docstrings.
  * Support help for the current command scope.
  * Support child-command discovery.
  * Provide useful suggestions for unknown commands.

* [/] Implement developer traceback controls.

  * Add the planned `--dev-traceback` flag.
  * Keep normal user-facing errors concise.
  * Show detailed traceback information only when explicitly requested.

* [ ] Implement the output/printer interface.

  * Centralize terminal output.
  * Support normal output.
  * Support warnings.
  * Support errors.
  * Prepare for colors and formatting.
  * Avoid direct terminal output from command implementations.

* [/] Finalize project-path discovery required by the foundation.

  * Resolve the current project root.
  * Resolve paths relative to the project root.
  * Establish the path model required by later project commands.

* [ ] Add command aliases.

  * Define alias metadata.
  * Resolve aliases during command lookup.
  * Detect alias conflicts.

* [ ] Finalize command-specific metadata/help behavior.

* [ ] Complete remaining generic file utilities required by the foundation.

  * Directory helpers.
  * File metadata.
  * Modification time.
  * File size.
  * Safe read helpers.

* [ ] Expand foundation tests after the execution model is finalized.

  * Global flags.
  * Child routing.
  * Command loading.
  * Exit codes.
  * Help/version behavior.
  * Error rendering.
  * Developer traceback behavior.

* [-] Implement feature ID generation and version management as part of SHP-000.

  * Moved to later project/release management work.

* [-] Implement Markdown utilities as part of SHP-000.

  * Moved to SHP-001/project-level functionality.

* [-] Implement `.shipyard` project management as part of SHP-000.

  * Moved to SHP-001.

---

## [SHP-001] Initialize a Shipyard Project

> **Goal:** Turn an existing directory into a Shipyard-managed project and establish the project's `.shipyard` structure and managed files.

* [ ] Implement the `shipyard init` command.

* [ ] Implement project structure detection.

  * Detect `README.md`.
  * Detect `ROADMAP.md`.
  * Detect `CHANGELOG.md`.
  * Detect `ideas/`.
  * Detect `.shipyard/`.

* [ ] Create missing project files and directories.

  * `README.md`
  * `ROADMAP.md`
  * `CHANGELOG.md`
  * `ideas/`
  * `.shipyard/`

* [ ] Validate the existing project structure.

* [ ] Repair missing or invalid Shipyard-managed files where possible.

* [ ] Initialize the `.shipyard` directory.

* [ ] Create and initialize the project registry inside `.shipyard`.

* [ ] Register managed project files within `.shipyard`.

* [ ] Implement managed-path resolution for project files.

* [ ] Implement the Markdown utilities required by project initialization.

  * Read Markdown.
  * Write Markdown.
  * Parse Markdown.
  * Edit Markdown sections.

* [ ] Use an existing Markdown/CommonMark library for parsing rather than implementing a complete Markdown parser.

* [ ] Build a thin Shipyard Markdown layer for locating and safely modifying Shipyard-managed sections.

* [ ] Update `.gitignore`.

  * Append recommended Shipyard entries.
  * Optionally allow the user to ignore the `ideas/` directory.

* [ ] Detect Git repositories.

* [ ] Provide Git/repository-specific recommendations after initialization.

* [ ] Display an initialization summary.

  * Files created.
  * Files already present.
  * Files registered.
  * Recommendations.
  * Suggested next commands.

* [ ] Validate the initialized project.

* [ ] Report remaining initialization issues.

* [ ] Write unit tests for `shipyard init`.

  * Fresh directory.
  * Existing project.
  * Partially initialized project.
  * Missing files.
  * Existing conflicting files.
  * Existing `.shipyard`.
  * Git repository detection.
  * Re-running `shipyard init`.

